This folder contains: 

(1) The CSV files that spec the omopdb data model:
    fields.csv, tables.csv, common_fields.csv
These files are used to generate the Pydantic model definitions.

(2) In subdir custom_templates, Jinja templates that are used to format the output file 
(see below). datamodel-code-generator uses Jinja to template all of its output and we
have made slight modifications to accommodate our needs.

(3) In subdir generated_outputs, the following outputs will be generated:
    (a) omop_pydantic_model_defs_{ts}.py.txt: python code containing class defs
        for the OMOP models that need to be cut/pasted into gen_epix.omopdb.domain.model.omop.
        In addition a version a json_schema is output, and is used in the process, but 
        nothing further needs to be done with this file. It can be inspected for fun.
    (b) omop_crud_command_class_defs_{tx}.py.txt: python code containing CRUD commands
        that need to be cut/pasted into gen_epix.omopdb.domain.command.crud 
        (specifically the OMOP section)
    (c) omop_sqlalchemy_models_{ts}.pt.txt: python code containing SQLAlchemy model 
        defs for the OMOP models, simply consisting of type hints. The content
        needs to be cut/pasted to gen_epix.omopdb.repositories.sa_model.omop.
    (d) omop_dummy_data_{ts}.xlsx: an Excel file that can be copied to 
        data/gen_epix/omopdb.src.omop.xlsx. This contains super-dumb data
        (not intended to be meaningful, but conform to data types)
        just for testing purposes.
    Here {ts} is a timestamp.

Explanation of the core files containing the OMOP spec.

Input files:
tables.csv: defines table-level specification; references tables only (no fields).
fields.csv: defines field-level specification, for all fields in each table.
common_fields.csv: defines field-level specification for fields that are used across multiple tables (e.g., provenance_id, source_trackback)

More details in comments, see BioBasePydanticModelGenerator.

Note that the code that generates the output files is in omop/omop; there are run
configurations for all necessary scripts.